  "title": "Tinnitus: The Sound That Isn't There",
  "emoji": "👁️",
  "color": "#A855F7",
  "description": "A 60-second lesson on Tinnitus: The Sound That Isn't There.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A constant ringing, buzzing, or hissing in your ears that nobody else can hear. This is tinnitus.",
      "visual": "🔔"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Tinnitus affects about 15% of adults. It's not a disease itself but a symptom of auditory system damage.",
      "visual": "📊"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "When hair cells die, the auditory cortex doesn't go quiet. It fills the silence with phantom noise.",
      "visual": "👻"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Tinnitus is a brain problem, not an ear problem. The sound is generated centrally, not peripherally.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Sound therapy and cognitive behavioral therapy can help the brain habituate and stop amplifying the phantom signal.",
      "visual": "🩺"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Tinnitus is the brain refusing to accept silence. It invents sound to fill the void.",
      "visual": "🔊"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "Where is the phantom sound of tinnitus generated?",
    "options": [
      "In the brain's auditory cortex",
      "In the ear canal",
      "In the eardrum"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
